    # 51..65: Step-by-step reasoning / problem solving (15)
    reasoning_data = [
        ("Step-by-step classification of changes",
         "Classify the following six changes as reversible or irreversible, providing logical justification for each: (a) Melting of ice cream, (b) Rusting of an iron gate, (c) Knitting a woolen sweater, (d) Burning dry leaves, (e) Inflating a bicycle tyre, (f) Baking a clay brick in a kiln.",
         "Student guesses based on whether they like or dislike the change.",
         "(a) Reversible, (b) Irreversible, (c) Reversible, (d) Irreversible, (e) Reversible, (f) Irreversible.",
         "We apply the definitive diagnostic test: Can the starting material be recovered in its original state by simple physical means without generating new chemical substances?",
         "1. (a) Melting ice cream: thermal phase change; cooling in a freezer solidifies it back -> Reversible.\n2. (b) Rusting iron: chemical reaction forming iron oxide; consumes iron metal permanently -> Irreversible.\n3. (c) Knitting sweater: yarn loops can be unraveled back into a single continuous ball of wool -> Reversible.\n4. (d) Burning leaves: combustion creates ash, smoke, and CO2; leaves cannot be reassembled -> Irreversible.\n5. (e) Inflating tyre: air enters under pressure; opening valve releases air back to original deflated size -> Reversible.\n6. (f) Baking clay brick: kiln heat chemically fuses minerals into ceramic terracotta permanently -> Irreversible.",
         "Classifying changes arbitrarily without applying the recovery test.",
         "Checking a two-way swinging door versus a one-way turnstile gate.",
         "None", "Which of the six changes above involve chemical transformations?",
         "Train students in systematic binary classification using the recovery test."),

        ("Fitting an iron rim to a wooden wheel",
         "A wooden cartwheel has a diameter of 100 cm. The blacksmith prepares an iron rim with a diameter of 98 cm. Explain step-by-step how the blacksmith fits this rim tightly onto the wheel.",
         "Student thinks the blacksmith uses a heavy hammer to smash the smaller iron rim over the wood.",
         "The blacksmith heats the 98 cm iron rim evenly over hot coals until thermal expansion widens its diameter to slightly over 100 cm; he slips it over the wooden wheel and rapidly douses it with cold water, causing the iron to contract and grip the wood firmly.",
         "Thermal expansion allows temporary, non-destructive enlargement of metal rings to achieve indestructible mechanical joints upon cooling.",
         "1. Initial dimension measurement: wooden wheel diameter = 100 cm; cold iron rim diameter = 98 cm (2 cm smaller).\n2. Heating stage: iron rim placed in a circular charcoal hearth and heated to red heat (~600°C).\n3. Thermal expansion: radial expansion enlarges the rim diameter beyond 100.5 cm.\n4. Assembly stage: glowing expanded rim is placed over the wooden rim using tongs.\n5. Quenching stage: cold water is poured immediately along the circumference.\n6. Thermal contraction: rapid cooling contracts the iron back toward 98 cm, clamping with immense compressive pressure onto the wood.",
         "Assuming mechanical force alone can stretch cold steel rings.",
         "Slipping a tight ring on your finger by lubricating it with soapy water, but here thermal expansion provides physical clearance.",
         "Diagram", "Draw the iron rim before heating, heated red-hot over coals, and being cooled with water on the wooden wheel.",
         "Explain thermal expansion calculations and metallurgical assembly."),

        ("Identifying changes in lighting a matchstick",
         "Break down the sequence of changes that occur when a safety matchstick is struck against the matchbox side.",
         "Student thinks lighting a match is a single instant event.",
         "Striking causes friction that converts mechanical energy into heat (physical change); heat ignites red phosphorus and potassium chlorate, triggering combustion of sulfur and wood into light, heat, smoke, and charred charcoal (irreversible chemical change).",
         "Match ignition involves a precise sequence of physical friction followed by multiple irreversible chemical oxidation reactions.",
         "1. Mechanical friction: match head rubs abrasive glass powder and red phosphorus on the box striking surface.\n2. Energy conversion: friction generates local heat of ~180°C (Physical change).\n3. Chemical triggering: heat converts tiny amount of red phosphorus into white phosphorus, which ignites.\n4. Flame propagation: spark ignites potassium chlorate and antimony trisulfide in the match head.\n5. Wood combustion: flame spreads to the wooden pine splint, burning cellulose into CO2, H2O vapour, and black carbon ash (Irreversible chemical change).\n6. Conclude: brief physical frictional trigger initiates rapid irreversible chemical combustion.",
         "Overlooking the initial physical frictional heating phase in combustion.",
         "A spark plug firing in a car engine: electrical spark (physical) ignites petrol vapour (chemical).",
         "Diagram", "Draw a matchstick head striking the friction strip, labeling the frictional heat step and the chemical combustion step.",
         "Guide step-by-step dissection of multi-stage chemical reactions."),

        ("Separating and recovering mixed wax and iron filings",
         "You are given a solid mixture of candle wax and iron filings stuck together. Describe a step-by-step experimental method to separate and recover both substances completely in their original states.",
         "Student thinks you have to pick filings with tweezers one by one out of solid wax.",
         "Step 1: Gently heat the beaker to melt the wax into a liquid (reversible change). Step 2: Pass a strong bar magnet wrapped in plastic wrap through the liquid wax to attract all iron filings out. Step 3: Cool the remaining liquid wax in an ice bath to solidify it back to clean solid wax.",
         "This method utilizes differences in physical properties: wax has a low melting point (~55°C), while iron is magnetic and has a high melting point (>1500°C).",
         "1. Property analysis: wax is non-magnetic with low melting point (55°C); iron is magnetic with high melting point (1538°C).\n2. Thermal stage: place mixture in a glass beaker inside a warm water bath (~70°C) until wax melts into clear liquid.\n3. Magnetic separation: lower a strong neodymium bar magnet sealed in a plastic bag into the liquid wax; iron filings jump and stick to the magnet.\n4. Extraction: lift magnet out and transfer filings into a dry petri dish.\n5. Wax recovery: pour remaining liquid wax into a mould and cool in refrigerator; wax solidifies back into clean solid block (Reversible phase change).\n6. Outcome: 100% recovery of both original components.",
         "Assuming solid mixtures cannot be separated without destroying the matrix.",
         "Melting chocolate chip cookies to extract chocolate, but here wax and iron are completely separated without damage.",
         "Virtual Lab", "Which separation tool is used in this procedure: a filter paper or a bar magnet?",
         "Integrate separation techniques with reversible phase transitions."),

        ("Recovery of water and salt from saline solution",
         "Describe the step-by-step laboratory procedure to prove that dissolving salt in water is a reversible change by recovering BOTH pure water and solid salt.",
         "Student thinks you can only get salt back and water is permanently lost into air.",
         "Set up a simple distillation apparatus: heat the salt water in a kettle until it boils into steam; route the steam through a delivery tube onto a metal plate cooled with ice cubes; condensed pure water drips into a beaker below, while dry salt crystals remain inside the kettle.",
         "Evaporation converts water to steam, leaving non-volatile salt behind; condensation cools steam back into liquid water. Both components are completely recovered in original mass.",
         "1. Apparatus assembly: glass distillation flask connected to a Liebig condenser with a cold-water jacket, collecting into an Erlenmeyer flask.\n2. Input: 100 mL of water containing 10 g of dissolved common salt.\n3. Heating phase: heat flask to 100°C; water boils into gaseous steam, leaving solid NaCl behind.\n4. Condensation phase: steam enters condenser; cooled inner walls extract latent heat, condensing steam back to liquid water droplets.\n5. Collection: pure distilled liquid water collects in the receiving flask.\n6. Residue inspection: white crystalline solid salt remains on the bottom of the heating flask.\n7. Mass balance check: 10 g salt + 100 mL pure water recovered.\n8. Conclude: 100% material recovery proves dissolution is a reversible physical change.",
         "Believing that evaporated liquid solvent is destroyed or non-recoverable.",
         "The natural water cycle: oceans evaporate salt water, clouds condense fresh rain, leaving ocean salt behind.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a distillation apparatus labeling the salt solution, steam, cooling condenser, pure water distillate, and dry salt residue.",
         "Enforce rigorous experimental proofs for physical reversibility."),

        ("Analyzing changes in bread making",
         "Trace the four major steps in bread making—(1) Mixing flour, water, and yeast into dough, (2) Kneading the dough, (3) Leaving dough in a warm place to rise, (4) Baking dough in an oven—and classify each step as reversible or irreversible.",
         "Student thinks all bread making steps are reversible until the last minute.",
         "(1) Irreversible (flour and water cannot be un-mixed easily; yeast activates), (2) Reversible (shaping/folding dough can be redone), (3) Irreversible (yeast ferments sugars into CO2 gas and alcohol), (4) Irreversible (heat denatures gluten and gelatinizes starches permanently).",
         "Bread making is an intricate sequence of physical shaping, biological fermentation, and chemical heat-induced baking.",
         "1. Step 1 (Mixing): flour starch hydrates with water, activating yeast enzymes -> Irreversible mechanical blending.\n2. Step 2 (Kneading): physical stretching aligns gluten strands into a smooth dough ball; reshaping is physically Reversible.\n3. Step 3 (Proofing/Rising): living yeast cells ferment maltose into carbon dioxide gas bubbles and ethanol, swelling dough volume -> Irreversible biochemical reaction.\n4. Step 4 (Baking at 200°C): heat sets protein network, traps gas pockets, evaporates alcohol, and creates golden Maillard crust -> Irreversible chemical transformation.\n5. Conclude: bread making progresses from physical manipulation to permanent biochemical cooking.",
         "Assuming yeast rising is just mechanical puffing like blowing up a balloon with a pump.",
         "Baking clay pottery: shaping is reversible, but firing in a furnace chemically transforms clay into ceramic.",
         "None", "Which gas produced by yeast causes the bread dough to swell up with spongy holes?",
         "Connect biological fermentation and thermal cooking to the reversible/irreversible framework."),

        ("Defrosting a refrigerator freezer",
         "A thick layer of white frost has built up inside a home freezer. Describe step-by-step why this frost formed, how to remove it reversibly, and why chipping it with a sharp knife is dangerous.",
         "Student thinks frost comes out of the plastic walls and can be chipped off with a knife safely.",
         "Warm humid room air enters whenever the door opens; moisture condenses and freezes into frost (reversible); defrost by unplugging the freezer and leaving doors open so frost melts into water naturally; chipping with a sharp knife can puncture the delicate aluminum cooling tubes containing pressurized refrigerant gas.",
         "Defrosting is a simple thermal phase change (solid frost -> liquid water) achieved safely by thermal warming rather than destructive mechanical force.",
         "1. Frost formation mechanism: opening freezer door admits warm atmospheric air containing water vapour.\n2. Reversible phase change: water vapour contacts -18°C cooling coils, subliming/condensing directly into solid ice crystals (frost).\n3. Safe defrosting protocol: unplug power cord, place towels and drip trays, prop freezer door wide open.\n4. Natural phase reversal: ambient room heat transfers into frost, melting ice at 0°C into liquid water drains.\n5. Mechanical hazard: using sharp knives or screwdrivers punctures soft aluminum evaporator coils, releasing toxic, flammable refrigerant gas and permanently wrecking the appliance.\n6. Conclude: rely on reversible thermal melting rather than violent mechanical hacking.",
         "Using violent mechanical tools to force a change that nature achieves gently with heat.",
         "Thawing frozen peas: soak in warm water rather than smashing them with a hammer.",
         "None", "Why does an open refrigerator door make the freezer develop thicker frost layers faster?",
         "Teach appliance safety and practical thermal phase management in domestic life."),

        ("Determining change in volume of water on freezing",
         "A student fills a rigid glass bottle completely to the brim with water, tightly seals the screw cap, and places it in the deep freezer overnight. Predict step-by-step what will happen and explain the scientific reason.",
         "Student thinks the bottle will just have cold water inside or that ice shrinks.",
         "The glass bottle will crack and shatter violently because water expands abnormally upon freezing, increasing its volume by approximately 9%; since the sealed rigid glass cannot stretch, the enormous expanding ice pressure bursts the bottle.",
         "Unlike almost all other liquids which contract upon freezing, water exhibits anomalous expansion: its hydrogen-bonded crystalline ice lattice occupies more volume than liquid water.",
         "1. Initial state: glass bottle filled 100% with liquid water at 20°C, zero air headspace, tightly capped.\n2. Cooling phase: temperature drops to 0°C; water begins freezing into solid ice.\n3. Anomalous expansion: open hexagonal crystal structure of ice requires ~9% greater volume than liquid water.\n4. Confined volume: rigid glass cannot expand to accommodate the extra volume.\n5. Pressure buildup: freezing water exerts immense hydraulic pressure exceeding 2000 atmospheres.\n6. Physical failure: tension exceeds the tensile strength of glass, causing the bottle to fracture with a loud crack.\n7. Safety conclusion: never freeze completely full liquid containers in sealed glass bottles.",
         "Assuming all liquids shrink and become denser upon freezing like molten wax.",
         "Potholes forming on winter roads: rainwater seeps into asphalt cracks, freezes, expands, and breaks up the road surface.",
         "Physical activity", "Fill a plastic bottle 90% full, mark water line with marker, freeze it, and observe the new ice level.",
         "Teach the anomalous expansion of water and its geological and domestic consequences."),

        ("Reversibility of a stretched steel spring",
         "A steel spring has an original length of 10 cm. When a 500 g mass is hung, it stretches to 14 cm; when removed, it returns to 10 cm. When a 5 kg mass is hung, it stretches to 35 cm; when removed, it stays at 28 cm. Explain step-by-step what happened.",
         "Student thinks the spring simply got tired or was made of weak plastic.",
         "Under 500 g, the spring deformed within its elastic limit (reversible change); under 5 kg, the immense force exceeded its elastic limit, causing permanent plastic deformation where metallic crystal planes slipped irreversibly.",
         "Materials exhibit a threshold called the elastic limit: below this threshold, deformations are fully reversible; above it, irreversible structural reorganization occurs.",
         "1. Test 1 (500 g mass): elongation = 14 - 10 = 4 cm. Removing load allows internal elastic forces to restore original 10 cm length -> 100% Reversible elastic deformation.\n2. Elastic limit threshold: the maximum stress a material can withstand without permanent shape change.\n3. Test 2 (5 kg mass): heavy load imposes extreme tensile stress exceeding the yield strength of the steel wire.\n4. Crystal slip: atomic planes in the steel slide past each other, creating permanent dislocation slips.\n5. Post-load measurement: spring length remains 28 cm (permanent set of 18 cm).\n6. Conclude: stretching within elastic limit is reversible; stretching beyond elastic limit is irreversible.",
         "Believing elastic materials can stretch infinitely without ever breaking or deforming permanently.",
         "Bending an aluminum paperclip slightly: springs back; bending it all the way back-and-forth: bends permanently and snaps.",
         "Graph", "Draw a graph of load vs spring length, marking the elastic region (reversible) and plastic region (irreversible).",
         "Introduce Hooke's law limits and the transition from reversible elasticity to irreversible plastic deformation."),

        ("Chemical test to prove irreversible changes: Limewater test",
         "When a student exhales through a drinking straw into a test tube of clear limewater, the liquid turns milky white. Describe step-by-step why this is an irreversible chemical change.",
         "Student thinks blowing air into water is just physical bubbling like blowing bubbles in soap.",
         "Carbon dioxide in exhaled breath chemically reacts with dissolved calcium hydroxide (limewater) to form insoluble calcium carbonate (chalk), turning the liquid milky white; this new chemical precipitate proves an irreversible chemical reaction occurred.",
         "Exhaling into limewater triggers a double-displacement chemical reaction that synthesizes a brand-new solid compound that cannot be un-mixed by physical stirring.",
         "1. Reactants: clear limewater solution [calcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)2] + exhaled carbon dioxide gas (CO2).\n2. Bubbling action: exhaled breath passes through straw into liquid.\n3. Chemical reaction equation: Ca(OH)2 (aqueous) + CO2 (gas) -> CaCO3 (insoluble solid precipitate) + H2O (liquid).\n4. Visual evidence: fine microscopic white particles of calcium carbonate suspend in water, scattering light and turning liquid milky.\n5. Reversibility test: you cannot turn the milky liquid back into clear limewater by filtering or blowing ordinary air.\n6. Conclude: formation of a new insoluble chemical compound (precipitate) is definitive proof of an irreversible chemical change.",
         "Thinking exhaled air is pure oxygen or that the milkiness is just trapped air bubbles.",
         "Adding lemon juice to clear tea: turns lighter yellow due to acid reaction, not just physical mixing.",
         "Virtual Lab", "What gas is specifically identified when clear limewater turns milky: oxygen, nitrogen, or carbon dioxide?",
         "Teach standard chemical reagent tests for gases and precipitation reactions."),

        ("Step-by-step repair of a broken wooden chair",
         "A wooden chair leg snaps cleanly in two. A carpenter fixes it by applying wood glue and clamping it tightly for 24 hours. Analyze step-by-step whether the snapping was reversible and whether the gluing is reversible.",
         "Student thinks gluing makes the wood grow back together like living bone.",
         "The snapping of the wood is an irreversible mechanical change; the gluing is also an irreversible chemical process because polyvinyl acetate (PVA) adhesive cures into a cross-linked polymer that cannot be un-cured back to liquid glue without destroying the wood.",
         "Repairing an object with adhesives joins broken surfaces mechanically, but does not reverse the original severance of woody xylem fibres.",
         "1. Step 1 (Snapping): mechanical stress breaks cellulose and lignin polymer fibres -> Irreversible structural failure.\n2. Step 2 (Adhesive application): liquid wood glue penetrates open cell lumens of both wood surfaces.\n3. Step 3 (Curing under pressure): water evaporates from glue; PVA polymers cross-link into a rigid insoluble plastic bond (Irreversible chemical polymerization).\n4. Forensic inspection: the joint is strong, but microscopically the wood fibres remain severed, bridged by a plastic adhesive film.\n5. Reversibility test: pulling the joint apart either tears new wood fibres or leaves glue residue; starting intact wooden leg is not restored.\n6. Conclude: snapping and gluing are both irreversible changes.",
         "Confusing functional repair with genuine scientific reversibility.",
         "Taping a torn page in a textbook: you can read the page, but the paper itself was not un-torn.",
         "None", "Does applying glue to broken wood turn the two pieces back into a single continuous wooden log?",
         "Distinguish between mechanical repair using bonding agents and scientific reversibility."),

        ("Comparing changes in water, wax, and wood upon heating",
         "Fill in a comparative table analyzing what happens when 10 g of (a) Ice, (b) Candle wax, and (c) Dry wood are heated from 20°C to 300°C in open crucibles.",
         "Student assumes all three substances burn into ash at 300°C.",
         "(a) Ice melts at 0°C to water, boils at 100°C to steam, leaving zero residue (reversible phase changes); (b) Wax melts at ~55°C, vaporizes, and burns in air to CO2 and H2O, leaving negligible ash (irreversible combustion); (c) Wood chars, smokes, catches fire, and burns completely into grey ash and gases (irreversible chemical combustion).",
         "Different substances respond to thermal energy based on their chemical composition: pure water undergoes physical phase changes, wax undergoes melting followed by combustion, and wood undergoes thermal pyrolysis and combustion.",
         "1. Substance (a) Ice/Water: 0°C melts -> 100°C boils completely into steam -> 100% reversible (condensable).\n2. Substance (b) Candle wax: 55°C melts into liquid wax (reversible) -> >200°C vaporizes and burns with oxygen into CO2 and smoke -> Irreversible.\n3. Substance (c) Dry wood: does not melt; at >250°C cellulose decomposes (pyrolysis) -> ignites into flame -> leaves mineral ash (C, K2CO3) -> Irreversible.\n4. Comparative insight: water has high chemical stability and phase reversibility; wood has complex organic polymers that decompose before melting.",
         "Believing all solid materials melt into liquid if heated enough.",
         "Heating sugar on a stove: melts first, then turns brown caramel, then burns to black carbon ash.",
         "Graph", "Draw temperature vs time curves for heating water vs heating wood up to 300°C.",
         "Contrast phase change stability with chemical thermal decomposition."),

        ("The blacksmith's blade hardening process (Quenching)",
         "Explain step-by-step why a blacksmith heats an iron knife blade red-hot and plunges it instantly into cold water (quenching), and classify the changes involved.",
         "Student thinks quenching is done to clean the blade from soot.",
         "Heating iron above 800°C alters its internal crystalline lattice structure; plunging it into cold water cools it so rapidly that carbon atoms are trapped in a strained crystal lattice (martensite), dramatically increasing hardness; reheating gently (tempering) is reversible to adjust toughness.",
         "Heat treatment of metals manipulates crystalline microstructure reversibly to achieve engineered mechanical hardness and flexibility.",
         "1. Heating phase: knife blade heated to bright cherry-red (~850°C) over forge coals; iron atoms shift from ferrite to austenite crystal structure.\n2. Rapid quenching: blade plunged vertically into a trough of cold water or oil.\n3. Microstructural trapping: instantaneous cooling prevents carbon atoms from diffusing out; crystal lattice distorts into rock-hard martensite.\n4. Mechanical outcome: blade becomes extremely hard, capable of holding a razor-sharp cutting edge, but brittle.\n5. Tempering phase: blacksmith reheats blade gently to 250°C and cools slowly, relieving internal stress and restoring tough flexibility.\n6. Conclude: metallurgical heat treatment utilizes controlled thermal cycles to reversibly manipulate crystal microstructure.",
         "Believing that cooling a hot metal always produces identical hardness.",
         "Tempering chocolate: heating and cooling to exact temperatures ensures smooth, glossy, snap-crisp chocolate bars.",
         "Diagram", "Draw a blacksmith's forge setup showing the glowing blade being dipped into a quenching water tub.",
         "Connect Class 6 thermal expansion concepts to advanced metallurgy and crystalline microstructure."),

        ("Sprouting seeds: physical or chemical?",
         "A student places dry gram (chana) seeds in a moist cloth for two days. White root-like sprouts emerge. Classify this change step-by-step.",
         "Student thinks the seeds just absorbed water like a sponge and sprouted mechanically.",
         "Sprouting of seeds is an irreversible biological and chemical change; although the initial swelling is physical water absorption (imbibition), the activation of enzymes, cellular respiration, cell division, and growth of embryonic radical and plumule cannot be reversed.",
         "Germination transforms stored dormant starches into active living tissues through irreversible biochemical synthesis and mitotic cell division.",
         "1. Stage 1 (Imbibition): dry chana seeds absorb water through the micropyle, swelling in size (Physical phase of water absorption).\n2. Stage 2 (Enzyme activation): hydration triggers gibberellin hormones, synthesizing amylase and protease enzymes.\n3. Stage 3 (Biochemical digestion): complex stored starches and proteins break down into soluble glucose and amino acids.\n4. Stage 4 (Mitotic growth): embryonic radical cells divide rapidly, bursting seed coat and elongating as a white sprout.\n5. Reversibility test: can you dehydrate the sprouted seedling in the sun to recover the original dormant, unsprouted seed? No; the sprout shrivels and dies.\n6. Conclude: seed germination is an irreversible biological and developmental change.",
         "Confusing physical swelling (water uptake) with living biological growth and cell division.",
         "A chicken egg hatching into a chick: once the chick emerges, it cannot be packed back into a raw egg.",
         "Physical activity", "Sprout moong seeds in a damp cotton pad over 3 days; record sprout length daily in millimeters.",
         "Distinguish physical imbibition from irreversible biological germination."),

        ("Step-by-step analysis of milk curdling with lemon juice",
         "When a chef boils milk and squeezes fresh lemon juice into it, the milk curdles instantly into paneer (cottage cheese) and watery whey. Analyze this change step-by-step.",
         "Student thinks lemon juice just coloured the milk or separated layers like oil and water.",
         "Citric acid from lemon juice lowers the pH of hot milk; this acidity neutralizes negative electric charges on casein protein micelles, causing them to clump together into solid white curds (paneer) while separating green watery whey—an irreversible chemical coagulation.",
         "Acid-induced protein denaturation permanently precipitates casein into cheese. No physical or chemical method can turn paneer and whey back into smooth liquid milk.",
         "1. Fresh milk state: colloidal suspension where casein protein micelles carry negative surface charges, repelling each other and staying suspended.\n2. Heat application: milk brought to gentle boil (~90°C), destabilizing protein hydration shells.\n3. Acid addition: fresh lemon juice introduces citric acid molecules (H+ ions).\n4. Charge neutralization: positive H+ ions neutralize negative surface charges on casein micelles.\n5. Coagulation: uncharged casein proteins attract each other, clumping into insoluble white curd curds (paneer).\n6. Separation: curds are strained through a muslin cloth, collecting solid paneer and liquid whey.\n7. Reversibility check: mixing paneer and whey together does not restore fresh milk.\n8. Conclude: irreversible chemical protein coagulation.",
         "Assuming curdling is just mechanical filtration of existing clumps.",
         "Adding acid to paint causes the pigment to coagulate into solid sludge.",
         "Diagram", "Draw milk before adding lemon juice (dispersed micelles) and after adding lemon juice (coagulated curd and clear whey).",
         "Teach colloidal chemistry and acid-induced protein coagulation using traditional paneer making.")
    ]
